WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.566
Hello. I'm Ron Vale

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:15.832
and today I have the pleasure to be

00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:18.832
speaking with Dr. Christiane Nüsslein-Volhard

00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:20.599
and Dr. Eric Wieschaus

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.732
who worked together as junior faculty

00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:26.066
between 1978 and 1981

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:28.499
and made historic discoveries about genes

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.899
that are involved in controlling

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.432
embryonic development. Work that lead

00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:34.799
to the Nobel Prize in 1995.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.499
So thank you very much for joining us

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.332
today and perhaps we can begin

00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:40.799
by describing some of the work

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.032
that you did and the discoveries that you made.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:46.666
Nüsslein-Volhard: I started working with flies with the idea to

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:54.066
find morphogens by mutations in their genes.

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:57.266
At the time, people were trying to isolate

00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.266
factors which would alter or change

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:04.666
embryonic development and they did that

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:08.866
by cutting and slicing experiments, essentially.

00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:12.732
Transplantation. Centrifugations. All these things.

00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:15.866
And all these experiments had lots of artifacts

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:19.566
and at the time, in our institute, they were

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.799
people who did genetics, in bacteria,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.566
trying to identify genes involved in

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:27.999
DNA replication and I figured if

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.866
one would try the same methods

00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:34.399
to development: making a mutation

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.732
in a gene which is really important

00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:38.299
for embryonic development

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.466
then you should see it in

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.666
phenotype and the gene would

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:48.199
be encoding something we are looking for.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.899
I mean, drosophila genetics

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.466
had been done but mostly on

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:53.032
the structures of the adult fly.

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:54.566
Only very few people looked

00:01:54.567 --> 00:01:56.566
at embryos at the time.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.266
And Eric was one of them.

00:01:58.267 --> 00:01:58.932
Wieschaus: Yeah.

00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:01.599
Nüsslein-Volhard: I met Eric in Walter Gehring's lab

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.299
in Basel where he was already

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.966
finishing his thesis on

00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:08.266
Drosophila embryology

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:11.366
and I found that extremely exciting

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:12.432
and learned a lot from him.

00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:13.866
And then we joined later.

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:17.432
Wieschaus: Yeah. I think you've,

00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:19.699
your sense of the problem was a

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.066
bit more, was actually really directed

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:24.599
towards morphogens. Whereas

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.266
I came into the field, I think,

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:32.299
because I had, as an embryologist,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.832
didn't have the molecular training,

00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:36.932
even the genetics training,

00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:39.032
In a certain sense, I had.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:40.466
I worked on flies all my life

00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:43.866
but what really fascinated me was

00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:47.799
watching embryos develop,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.632
and that these things happen

00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:56.099
that are reproducible and

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.532
mysterious but in some way had,

00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:03.866
by that time, we knew, had to

00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:07.032
depend on specific proteins

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.666
and therefore specific genes.

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:10.399
If genes are important

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.999
for morphogens, if they are important for

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.232
everything an embryo does,

00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:16.232
then if you knocked.

00:03:16.233 --> 00:03:17.699
If you set up a program where

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.799
you are going to randomly

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.132
mutagenize genes, knock them out,

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:27.432
in set of little inbred lines because flies are diploid,

00:03:27.433 --> 00:03:30.166
and then look at the consequences of

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:34.132
thousands and thousands and thousands of mutations

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:37.732
to identify which mutations

00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:41.332
in which genes therefore affect processes

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:44.766
that are discrete and understandable in the embryo.

00:03:44.767 --> 00:03:45.832
Just about what we did.

00:03:45.833 --> 00:03:48.899
We struggled to do it for several years

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:49.832
to figure out how we could do it.

00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:51.199
Nüsslein-Volhard: It took some time until we

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.199
focused in on this project.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.999
Wieschaus: And how to do it.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.032
Because people had that mutagenesis.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:03:59.799
Ed Lewis had figured out how to use EMS

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.866
to induce mutations. That was a huge, wonderful thing

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:04.799
about flies. People had worked on them

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.766
so we knew how to do stuff.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:11.466
But how to do it on the scale that

00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:16.266
we thought was necessary to actually

00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:18.999
to get enough of the pieces of the puzzle

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.632
to get a picture of what was

00:04:21.633 --> 00:04:23.232
really happening was hard.

00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:24.832
Nüsslein-Volhard: One of the things we did

00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:27.366
which contrasted the previous work

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:30.566
is that we figured that the result of

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:32.099
embryonic development would the larva

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.632
and not the fly so we should

00:04:33.633 --> 00:04:36.066
look at larval structure and not at fly structures.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.632
And this is what he did in his

00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:39.566
thesis and then we turned to

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:41.432
developing methods.

00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:43.499
How you could really see the segments

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.566
in a living embryo and also in a

00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:47.532
prepared embryo and use

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:49.099
this pattern of the cuticle

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.832
which is displaying the segmentation

00:04:51.833 --> 00:04:54.899
as landmarks for identifying mutants.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.999
We established 12,000 inbred lines

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.899
which had on average had 1 or 2 mutations

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.232
in them and looked at the consequences in the

00:05:06.233 --> 00:05:11.799
embryonic pattern in the larva, in the cuticle

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.132
and we discovered 120 new genes

00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:19.566
which when mutated, caused strong

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:24.932
changes. Distinguishable, visable changes in the pattern

00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:27.232
which was a small fraction of the entire

00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:29.266
genome, about 1% or so.

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:30.432
Wieschaus: So we were lucky there.

00:05:30.433 --> 00:05:33.166
Nüsslein-Volhard: Most of the genes, when mutated,

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:35.399
don't cause any morphological

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.299
visible alterations. Embryos just die.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.799
They look normal. But we'd pulled out

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.332
those that did not look normal.

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:45.366
Wieschaus: After that initial screen,

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:50.466
to bring most of those loci to a point of

00:05:50.467 --> 00:05:55.266
publication still took 3 or 4 years.

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:58.099
To go from, we did the screens,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.332
we published an initial paper in Nature

00:06:00.333 --> 00:06:03.632
on the small class that affected segmentation

00:06:03.633 --> 00:06:08.899
but the broad group of 120, 130

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.699
genes that affected all aspects of

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.899
embryonic development, to map,

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.166
to characterize, to assign to complementation groups

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:21.232
all of that took 3 more years.

00:06:21.233 --> 00:06:23.332
Vale: So the two of you worked very closely

00:06:23.333 --> 00:06:25.366
together during this time

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:28.732
and I'm curious: what features of

00:06:28.733 --> 00:06:30.766
about you as scientists were similar

00:06:30.767 --> 00:06:34.432
that allowed you to really synergize in the

00:06:34.433 --> 00:06:36.032
type of work you did and

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:37.466
in the discoveries that you made?

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:40.632
:Nüsslein-Volhard: We share one feature which is

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:41.932
important. We are very good

00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:50.232
observers, both. I think, hardly anyone who can...

00:06:50.233 --> 00:06:52.966
Wieschaus: It was terrible. It was wonderful but I mean

00:06:52.967 --> 00:06:54.799
Nüsslein-Volhard: We really matched up on

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.432
this and it's rare that you match up

00:06:57.433 --> 00:06:59.199
with other people in this special

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.799
quality that you really

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.332
recognize minute details

00:07:03.333 --> 00:07:05.899
and I think we are equal

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.766
in this respect and it was

00:07:07.767 --> 00:07:10.499
maybe really exceptional that

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.299
two people who are so

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.399
good in observing shared

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.432
this project. We had this

00:07:17.433 --> 00:07:19.032
dissection scope where we looked

00:07:19.033 --> 00:07:21.466
in from two sides on the same

00:07:21.467 --> 00:07:22.732
embryos and then we

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:25.966
and one said "Ah look. Look at this!"

00:07:25.967 --> 00:07:28.166
and then "Yes I've seen it already." This kind of thing.

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:31.566
We really, we had some phenotypes

00:07:31.567 --> 00:07:34.299
which were so subtle and we both

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.566
saw them and then we discussed

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:38.399
is it worth keeping?

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.766
Is it not worth keeping?

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:41.232
We had to kind of have these cut off

00:07:41.233 --> 00:07:44.299
criteria where one bristle too little or

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.499
one hair too curled?

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.532
Is it enough to be significant?

00:07:49.533 --> 00:07:53.832
We did agree in most cases.

00:07:53.833 --> 00:07:55.699
Wieschaus: There was of course a kind of

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:58.566
not quite a comp...

00:07:58.567 --> 00:08:00.032
well maybe yes, a competitive

00:08:00.033 --> 00:08:02.666
element in that because we were

00:08:02.667 --> 00:08:05.966
so equally matched in the ability to see

00:08:05.967 --> 00:08:09.699
phenotypes that were phenotypes

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.132
that, you know, you have this huge

00:08:12.133 --> 00:08:13.566
mound of slides and you are looking

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:15.532
through them one at a time

00:08:15.533 --> 00:08:17.266
and most of slides are garbage

00:08:17.267 --> 00:08:20.266
because you just did embryos

00:08:20.267 --> 00:08:22.232
from mutagenized stocks and so

00:08:22.233 --> 00:08:23.999
the whole trick was to sort,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.866
put the slide on and look at

00:08:25.867 --> 00:08:27.466
it and recognize that there was a

00:08:27.467 --> 00:08:29.632
quarter of the embryos that showed a

00:08:29.633 --> 00:08:33.332
discrete phenotype. It could have

00:08:33.333 --> 00:08:35.299
happened that either one of us was,

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.466
despite of our observational abilities,

00:08:38.467 --> 00:08:41.166
could have been temporarily distracted

00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:43.599
and the other would see the

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.332
phenotype first.

00:08:45.333 --> 00:08:48.699
Nüsslein-Volhard: That kept us awake.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:49.466
It was very important.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:54.399
It's a tough job and it is extremely tiring.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.166
When you look all the time.

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:56.466
It's like when you are in a museum

00:08:56.467 --> 00:08:58.132
and you are there in the museum

00:08:58.133 --> 00:09:00.366
and you are content. But when someone

00:09:00.367 --> 00:09:01.166
goes with you and says

00:09:01.167 --> 00:09:02.599
"Look at this, look at this"

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.532
then its much, much easier.

00:09:04.533 --> 00:09:06.432
Wieschaus: And you did actually

00:09:06.433 --> 00:09:09.199
develop a special relationship

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.799
to those loci that are mutant lines

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.532
that you saw, that I saw, first

00:09:16.533 --> 00:09:20.199
and she didn't see until three seconds later

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.699
on the slide. So those became really

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.166
like they were mine and you also have.

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:33.232
These will stay secret. These are genes that

00:09:33.233 --> 00:09:35.599
are out in the public domain now and so

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:36.732
you don't necessarily want to know

00:09:36.733 --> 00:09:40.032
who are Janni's special children and who

00:09:40.033 --> 00:09:42.032
are my special children in that respect.

00:09:42.033 --> 00:09:44.599
Vale: So Janni, I was struck by one of the

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.166
the quotes in your Nobel Prize biography,

00:09:47.167 --> 00:09:51.666
which said that "We realized the screening

00:09:51.667 --> 00:09:54.166
for embryonic mutants would be very rewarding

00:09:54.167 --> 00:09:56.332
and that we were the only people

00:09:56.333 --> 00:09:57.732
in the world that could do it, "

00:09:57.733 --> 00:09:59.999
and I’m just curious what that

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.566
meant and if you could elaborate on that?

00:10:02.567 --> 00:10:04.332
Nüsslein-Volhard: True. No?

00:10:04.333 --> 00:10:08.066
Wieschaus: At the time, certainly

00:10:08.067 --> 00:10:11.932
but that is the kind of statement

00:10:11.933 --> 00:10:14.732
you have to justify so now is your moment.

00:10:14.733 --> 00:10:16.099
[Nüsslein-Volhard] You mean it's too arrogant

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:17.466
in such a biography?

00:10:17.467 --> 00:10:20.232
No, it was strange. Listen Ron,

00:10:20.233 --> 00:10:23.866
I remember when we had done the screen

00:10:23.867 --> 00:10:25.599
and we were thrilled and we thought

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.032
"Shesh this is exciting" and we had

00:10:27.033 --> 00:10:30.499
a poster of 48 pictures of

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.766
these mutants on the

00:10:31.767 --> 00:10:34.032
second chromosome and

00:10:34.033 --> 00:10:35.532
they were extremely significant,

00:10:35.533 --> 00:10:37.332
the phenotypes, and

00:10:37.333 --> 00:10:39.232
we knew that we couldn't

00:10:39.233 --> 00:10:40.299
interpret them properly but

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:41.599
we knew each of them has

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.899
to be explained and put

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:45.399
into some framework before

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.499
we understand how embryos are made.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.566
So it was a great achievement

00:10:50.567 --> 00:10:53.166
we were sort of proud about it, sure.

00:10:53.167 --> 00:10:55.999
And we presented this in a

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.432
drosophila meeting where there

00:10:59.433 --> 00:11:01.299
the biggies of the field were

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.832
present and they stood in

00:11:03.833 --> 00:11:07.366
front of this poster and "Oh, what's this?"

00:11:07.367 --> 00:11:09.666
and then they turned around

00:11:09.667 --> 00:11:11.066
and went away.

00:11:11.067 --> 00:11:13.166
They couldn't understand it.

00:11:13.167 --> 00:11:14.966
They didn't even

00:11:14.967 --> 00:11:20.599
They had no…they didn't even say "Wow! Oh!"

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:25.966
I mean some hostile. Others

00:11:25.967 --> 00:11:28.099
were completely insecure,

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:29.632
didn't know what to say

00:11:29.633 --> 00:11:31.099
And this was the response.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:36.032
And this was sort of...

00:11:36.033 --> 00:11:37.699
This is why we say "Well,

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:38.832
only we could have done it."

00:11:38.833 --> 00:11:40.199
I mean, if no one can

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.932
understand it, it means no one else

00:11:42.933 --> 00:11:45.166
could've done it too.

00:11:45.167 --> 00:11:46.432
Vale: So I gather that the two of

00:11:46.433 --> 00:11:49.799
you first met in Walter Gehring’s lab although

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.732
you only overlapped for a short period

00:11:51.733 --> 00:11:53.799
of time. Was there something

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:55.266
about when you first meet

00:11:55.267 --> 00:11:56.766
each other that you realized you had

00:11:56.767 --> 00:12:01.099
similar interests or similar intellectual curiosities?

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:02.599
Nüsslein-Volhard: So I went to work

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.199
in Walter Gerhring's lab actually

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.666
Wieschaus: In January.

00:12:05.667 --> 00:12:10.566
Nüsslein-Volhard: In January 1975

00:12:10.567 --> 00:12:13.032
and Eric had just finished his

00:12:13.033 --> 00:12:16.132
thesis and that was full of

00:12:16.133 --> 00:12:18.732
people who came to work with

00:12:18.733 --> 00:12:20.966
drosophila without having

00:12:20.967 --> 00:12:22.799
worked on drosophila before

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.032
and Eric was one of the

00:12:24.033 --> 00:12:25.132
very few people in this

00:12:25.133 --> 00:12:26.432
lab who knew something

00:12:26.433 --> 00:12:27.732
about embryos. He

00:12:27.733 --> 00:12:28.999
knew something embryos

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.499
and it was another woman,

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:31.766
Jeanette Holden, who

00:12:31.767 --> 00:12:33.332
knew drosophila genetics

00:12:33.333 --> 00:12:36.966
and so I tried to get as much

00:12:36.967 --> 00:12:38.199
out of them as I could.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.532
Jeanette left after half a year

00:12:40.533 --> 00:12:43.866
later and Eric left a couple

00:12:43.867 --> 00:12:45.766
months. He had already started to

00:12:45.767 --> 00:12:47.732
move to Zurich but I tried to

00:12:47.733 --> 00:12:49.166
and he came back because he

00:12:49.167 --> 00:12:49.932
had a collaboration with

00:12:49.933 --> 00:12:52.266
people and I just tried to get

00:12:52.267 --> 00:12:53.866
as much knowledge out of him

00:12:53.867 --> 00:12:56.399
as I possible could and

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.932
that was a time for me, for which I was

00:12:58.933 --> 00:13:01.432
eagerly picking up whatever I

00:13:01.433 --> 00:13:02.999
could get and it was

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.499
so exciting. I dreamt of flies

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:06.699
when I closed my eyes I saw

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:11.299
fly embryos.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:12.566
Wieschaus: We used to met and eat

00:13:12.567 --> 00:13:15.132
dinner together and talk

00:13:15.133 --> 00:13:18.466
about stuff. I think we talked about

00:13:18.467 --> 00:13:20.999
science. We talked about art.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.666
I was, you know because I grew up in

00:13:22.667 --> 00:13:23.632
in Alabama, I was like...

00:13:23.633 --> 00:13:24.132
Nüsslein-Volhard: I knew nothing about

00:13:24.133 --> 00:13:26.532
imaginal discs. I had a hard time

00:13:26.533 --> 00:13:29.132
understanding your thesis and was so...

00:13:29.133 --> 00:13:34.066
Wieschaus: I had a hard time understanding my thesis!

00:13:34.067 --> 00:13:36.766
Nüsslein-Volhard: But we had common interests too. I

00:13:36.767 --> 00:13:39.932
I mean music and drawing and arts.

00:13:39.933 --> 00:13:42.666
So both of you then did your postdocs

00:13:42.667 --> 00:13:45.266
and then you both got hired

00:13:45.267 --> 00:13:47.032
at pretty much the same time at

00:13:47.033 --> 00:13:49.132
the European Molecular Biology Lab.

00:13:49.133 --> 00:13:51.999
Was that just a coincidence or was

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:53.799
there some planned synergy

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:55.732
that brought the two of you together?

00:13:55.733 --> 00:13:56.399
Nüsslein-Volhard: I was looking

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:57.732
for a job after two years in

00:13:57.733 --> 00:14:00.332
Walter's lab. I had an EBMO fellowship

00:14:00.333 --> 00:14:03.132
which run out and he and I

00:14:03.133 --> 00:14:05.999
didn't hit off together too well

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:11.299
actually and I wanted to leave but I

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:13.066
didn't have a place to go

00:14:13.067 --> 00:14:15.432
so I applied to the EMBL,

00:14:15.433 --> 00:14:17.832
which was new at the time,

00:14:17.833 --> 00:14:19.632
it was the second year or so,

00:14:19.633 --> 00:14:24.699
and I didn't get a job because

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.399
people said "Well maybe she

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:27.532
can't do that alone,

00:14:27.533 --> 00:14:29.866
she is just started working with

00:14:29.867 --> 00:14:33.199
flies and in case some other fly

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:34.299
people come to the lab

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:36.832
yes, maybe but not alone."

00:14:36.833 --> 00:14:41.266
And then I first got a

00:14:41.267 --> 00:14:42.899
another fellowship and went to

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.666
Freiburg and spent a year in

00:14:45.667 --> 00:14:46.999
Klaus Sander's lab and

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.632
embryologist, insect embryologist,

00:14:49.633 --> 00:14:52.532
and then I got the offer from the

00:14:52.533 --> 00:14:54.699
EMBL and they had in the meantime

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.899
asked Eric. You had been asked hadn't you?

00:14:57.900 --> 00:14:59.166
Wieschaus: I was asked to...

00:14:59.167 --> 00:15:00.232
Nüsslein-Volhard: You hadn't even applied.

00:15:00.233 --> 00:15:00.699
Wieschaus: I hadn't applied.

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:01.432
Nüsslein-Volhard: He didn't have to

00:15:01.433 --> 00:15:03.166
apply. He just was asked

00:15:03.167 --> 00:15:05.032
would you be interested in

00:15:05.033 --> 00:15:07.799
coming to the EMBL.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.266
What a luxury!

00:15:09.267 --> 00:15:11.066
Wieschaus: Yeah, it was pretty cool.

00:15:11.067 --> 00:15:13.632
Nüsslein-Volhard: I was struggling and I dreamt of

00:15:13.633 --> 00:15:15.532
being out of unemployment and

00:15:15.533 --> 00:15:17.066
didn't know what to do and I wrote

00:15:17.067 --> 00:15:18.932
letters to people I didn't really

00:15:18.933 --> 00:15:20.732
respect and begged them to

00:15:20.733 --> 00:15:21.999
give me a job. It was really

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.632
tough because I had

00:15:23.633 --> 00:15:25.199
started working with flies

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.866
only when I was the age of 32.

00:15:27.867 --> 00:15:28.999
I had done a long PhD,

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.032
a good, interesting PhD, on

00:15:31.033 --> 00:15:32.966
bacteria genetics, no, bacterial

00:15:32.967 --> 00:15:35.566
molecular biology and then

00:15:35.567 --> 00:15:38.232
switched to flies with great

00:15:38.233 --> 00:15:40.499
enthusiasm but in a short time

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.599
in the lab and in Walter's lab, I had

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.066
published a paper in contrast to

00:15:46.067 --> 00:15:47.232
all these other brilliant

00:15:47.233 --> 00:15:49.632
postdocs who took them much

00:15:49.633 --> 00:15:50.666
longer to publish something

00:15:50.667 --> 00:15:53.166
but I didn't get a job for that

00:15:53.167 --> 00:15:55.599
because this was not a field for which

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:59.199
it was not a big field and people were...nah.

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:01.032
I didn't get credit for what

00:16:01.033 --> 00:16:02.366
I had done before so

00:16:02.367 --> 00:16:03.499
for me it was a tough time

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:05.699
I must say. And then

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.166
when I got the job at EMBL

00:16:07.167 --> 00:16:08.632
I was of course thrilled and

00:16:08.633 --> 00:16:10.866
together with Eric, oh great fun, yes!

00:16:10.867 --> 00:16:12.632
But they treated us in a very

00:16:12.633 --> 00:16:16.132
"maybe we can take this risk

00:16:16.133 --> 00:16:17.666
it's not so expensive. let's take

00:16:17.667 --> 00:16:19.266
these two guys and put them in

00:16:19.267 --> 00:16:20.832
the smallest lab" and a very

00:16:20.833 --> 00:16:22.766
small, we had a...

00:16:22.767 --> 00:16:25.166
an office space together

00:16:25.167 --> 00:16:27.032
two by two meters.

00:16:27.033 --> 00:16:31.532
Wieschaus: We discussed a lot.

00:16:31.533 --> 00:16:34.632
Nüsslein-Volhard: But not in this phase.

00:16:34.633 --> 00:16:36.332
The lab was really so small that

00:16:36.333 --> 00:16:41.699
it was a bit hard but we didn't

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.532
bother to much and we just tried to

00:16:43.533 --> 00:16:44.932
to get our microscopes working and

00:16:44.933 --> 00:16:47.099
going and we got a technician,

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:49.099
shared technician, and a

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:53.499
girl who took care of the

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.566
flies and then we had a good set up.

00:16:56.567 --> 00:16:59.366
we had no students. It was also no

00:16:59.367 --> 00:17:01.132
discussion of whether you get

00:17:01.133 --> 00:17:03.099
PhD students. We just were

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:04.566
on our own. So we were

00:17:04.567 --> 00:17:10.032
a two headed group without a body, essentially.

00:17:10.033 --> 00:17:11.266
Wieschaus: Essentially for us,

00:17:11.267 --> 00:17:14.332
being in the lab, having the

00:17:14.333 --> 00:17:15.899
facilities that were available

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.999
at the EMBL, not having to worry about

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.732
teaching, not having worry about

00:17:20.733 --> 00:17:23.866
money, being in this small group

00:17:23.867 --> 00:17:26.232
where the most obvious thing for us

00:17:26.233 --> 00:17:28.966
to do was to push forward

00:17:28.967 --> 00:17:31.432
this common experiment.

00:17:31.433 --> 00:17:34.132
That was enough! I mean

00:17:34.133 --> 00:17:38.099
there we were and we could talk.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:42.666
We talked with each other a lot

00:17:42.667 --> 00:17:48.466
and then it we went off and you knew

00:17:48.467 --> 00:17:50.732
other people. It wasn't totally enough

00:17:50.733 --> 00:17:52.099
and it wasn't that we were totally

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:55.866
isolated but the reason why it worked

00:17:55.867 --> 00:17:57.832
was that we were intellectually

00:17:57.833 --> 00:17:59.032
dependent on each other.

00:17:59.033 --> 00:18:02.132
Vale: So I was wondering how you're

00:18:02.133 --> 00:18:05.299
experience might be relevant

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:08.266
for young people today who

00:18:08.267 --> 00:18:09.999
are obviously trying to start their own careers

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.266
and achieve their own independence

00:18:12.267 --> 00:18:13.832
but also want to collaborate with

00:18:13.833 --> 00:18:14.966
one another?

00:18:14.967 --> 00:18:16.199
Wieschaus: The good thing about

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:17.299
obviously the good thing about

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:18.699
collaborations is when they work

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:21.032
is that you do have

00:18:21.033 --> 00:18:22.899
two people, that if they are

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:26.499
really good and they, even

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:30.066
if it's not truly yin and yang,

00:18:30.067 --> 00:18:38.099
complementary, it is an overlapping

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.932
synergism that is hard to predict

00:18:39.933 --> 00:18:41.499
but I think that's why you want to

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:45.232
why you are really lucky in your

00:18:45.233 --> 00:18:49.966
career if you are able to collaborate.

00:18:49.967 --> 00:18:51.299
Nüsslein-Volhard: And in this case,

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:53.399
I think that none of us could have done

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:57.766
this project alone and it got much richer

00:18:57.767 --> 00:18:59.932
with two of us. Also the quality of the

00:18:59.933 --> 00:19:01.666
analysis, I think, is not just

00:19:01.667 --> 00:19:05.199
maybe could have discovered

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.699
120 genes but how you describe them

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.299
and how you put them in order.

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:12.032
I think that was really much,

00:19:12.033 --> 00:19:14.566
much, much better with us

00:19:14.567 --> 00:19:17.899
two together compared to each of us

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:20.399
singularly and I think it's the best

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:24.166
project I’ve done in my life.

00:19:24.167 --> 00:19:26.166
Wieschaus: I have to obviously say

00:19:26.167 --> 00:19:56.833
that it was the great time in my own career.

